@page "/docs/tools/crud"

@* izzy add crud <Model> (catalog/crud in src/IzzyBlazor.Cli): the grid page it writes, how it reads the model,
   the store, the options, Pro's --store http (the .Client project and {Model}Api), and Pro's --mode pages (artifacts/izzyblazor-crud-design.md). *@

<ArticlePage Section="tools" Slug="crud">
    <Lead>
        <Code>izzy add crud</Code> creates a <Anchor Href="docs/components/data-grid">DataGrid</Anchor> page to display,
        add, edit and delete a model's records, for internal tools and admin screens. Name a class from your app; izzy
        reads it from the source, so the app doesn't have to build first.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Add the page</Heading>
        <Code Block Language="bash">izzy add crud Customer</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            This creates a page at <Code>/customers</Code>. The grid on it can search, sort and page the customers, and filters
            its choice columns (an enum, a yes or no) from their headers. Add and Edit open a dialog that checks the form
            against the model's attributes, and Delete asks before it deletes. A new customer's dates start at today.
        </TextBlock>
        @* The page izzy creates, live in a frame (Frame/CrudDemoFramePage.razor), at a desktop's or a phone's
           width. The switch reloads the page; data-site-return keeps the preview where it was on the screen. *@
        <div id="crud-demo" class="site-crud-demo" data-site-return>
            <TabNav Label="Preview width" Variant="TabsVariant.Segmented" Size="ControlSize.Sm">
                <TabLink Href="docs/tools/crud#crud-demo" Label="Desktop" Current="!Phone" />
                <TabLink Href="docs/tools/crud?width=phone#crud-demo" Label="Phone" Current="Phone" />
            </TabNav>
            @if (Phone)
            {
                <div class="site-block-phone">
                    <iframe class="site-block-frame" src="frame/cli/crud?scrollbar=none" data-site-frame title="The customers page at a phone's width"></iframe>
                </div>
            }
            else
            {
                <iframe class="site-crud-frame" src="frame/cli/crud" data-site-frame data-site-fit title="The customers page"></iframe>
            }
        </div>
        <TextBlock>
            The toolbar also has Columns and Export. Columns lets each person choose which columns to show, and remembers
            the choice in a cookie. Export downloads the rows as an Excel file, from an endpoint in
            <Code>Data/CustomerExport.cs</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            It works on static pages and on interactive pages. When the app has accounts, the user must log in to open it.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Where the records are kept</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            The page loads and saves records through an <Code>ICustomerStore</Code>. If one of your DbContexts has a
            <Code>DbSet&lt;Customer&gt;</Code>, the store uses it. Otherwise the records are kept in memory until the app
            stops. To choose for yourself, set <Code>--store</Code>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Labels and fields</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Each field's label comes from <Code>[Display(Name)]</Code>, or from the property's name split into words. A
            column's title comes from <Code>ShortName</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            A <Code>DateTime</Code> or <Code>DateTimeOffset</Code> with a time is entered and shown in UTC, and its label
            says so: "Opened (UTC)". To use another zone, give the field a <Code>TimeZone</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            Some properties don't get a field: a key the database creates, read-only properties and computed ones. Related
            records, such as a customer's orders, aren't included either. izzy lists each property it leaves out.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            A key people type in, such as an invoice number, needs the Pro pages. They ask for it when adding a record and
            show it read-only when editing.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Options</Heading>
        <Table Items="CrudOptions" Caption="izzy add crud options" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Option" Width="Length.Rem(14)"><code>@context!.Name</code></TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="What it does">@context!.Text</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>

        <Row Gap="3" Align="Align.Center" Wrap>
            <Heading Level="2">A page per task, with Pro</Heading>
            <Badge Variant="BadgeVariant.Outline">Planned</Badge>
        </Row>
        <TextBlock>
            The grid page does everything in one place and edits in a dialog. With IzzyBlazor Pro, <Code>--mode pages</Code>
            creates separate pages instead: a list with filters, a details page for each customer, and a form page that
            lists its errors at the top. Records with many fields have more room on pages of their own.
        </TextBlock>

        <Row Gap="3" Align="Align.Center" Wrap>
            <Heading Level="2">Pages that run in the browser, with Pro</Heading>
            <Badge Variant="BadgeVariant.Outline">Planned</Badge>
        </Row>
        <TextBlock>
            The pages read the store on the server, so izzy doesn't add them to an app whose pages run in WebAssembly.
            With IzzyBlazor Pro, <Code>--store http</Code> does, in any app with a <Code>.Client</Code> project. The
            pages, <Code>ICustomerStore</Code> and an <Code>HttpCustomerStore</Code> go into the
            <Code>.Client</Code> project. The server gets <Code>Data/CustomerApi.cs</Code>, an API at
            <Code>/api/customers</Code> over the DbContext, or over memory when no DbContext has the model.
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">izzy add crud Customer --store http</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            In the browser the grid's search, sort, filters and paging travel in the request's address, and the server
            runs them in the database. While the server draws the page first, it reads the server's store directly.
            The API asks for the same sign-in and policies as the pages. It sends only the fields the pages show, so a
            property izzy left out stays on the server. It checks what it is sent: the model's validation runs, and
            only the fields the form edits are saved.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            The model class has to be in the <Code>.Client</Code> project, where the pages can see it. The server
            project sees it there too. In an app whose pages are static by default, the pages get
            <Code>@@rendermode InteractiveAuto</Code>, or <Code>InteractiveWebAssembly</Code> when the app has no
            server interactivity.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">After the model changes</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Run the command again with <Code>--diff</Code> to see what would change. Then run it with
            <Code>--overwrite</Code> to update the files. See <Anchor Href="docs/tools/changes">Previewing changes</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    [SupplyParameterFromQuery(Name = "width")] private string? Width { get; set; }

    private bool Phone => Width == "phone";

    private sealed record OptionRow(string Name, string Text);

    private static readonly OptionRow[] CrudOptions =
    [
        new("--mode pages", "Pro, planned. Creates separate pages to list, show and edit the records, for records with many fields. Without it, izzy creates one grid page that edits in a dialog."),
        new("--name <Plural>", "Sets the folder, the page names and the page title. Defaults to the model's plural, such as Customers."),
        new("--singular <words>", "Sets what one record is called on the page, when it isn't the class name. For example, --singular \"team member\" for a Member class. To set the plural, use --plural."),
        new("--route <path>", "Sets the page's URL. Defaults to the name in kebab case, such as customers."),
        new("--store ef|memory", "Sets where the records are kept. ef needs a DbSet of the model. When two DbContexts have one, choose with --context."),
        new("--store http", "Pro, planned. For an app with a .Client project: writes the pages there and adds an API on the server for them to call."),
        new("--authorize", "Lets only signed-in people open the page. On by default when the app has accounts."),
        new("--view-policy <name>", "Lets only people the authorization policy allows open the page, such as ViewSales."),
        new("--edit-policy <name>", "Lets only people the policy allows add, edit and delete. Everyone else who can open the pages sees the records read-only."),
    ];
}
